    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a really hard time with my signals class. i get the formulas for the fourier transform but i dont really get the actual intuition. like how can one single wave just be made up of a bunch of other waves? can u explain it in a way that actually makes sense?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of a complex sound, like a chord played on a piano; it's not just one pure tone. The Fourier Transform essentially acts like a forensic tool, breaking that complex sound down into all the individual, simple sine and cosine waves (frequencies) that compose it. Each frequency tells you *how much* of that specific note is present in the original signal. By adding those pure sine waves back together at their correct strengths, you perfectly reconstruct the original complicated wave.",
