    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense for sound, but what about like... a messy electrical signal? it seems like there's so much noise and random stuff happening in the wave. how does the math actually \"see\" those specific frequencies hiding in there if they're all jumbled up?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For electrical signals, the Fourier Transform is powerful because it doesn't care about the *order* of the points in the wave; it just measures how much energy exists at each specific frequency across the entire time duration. Imagine sweeping a highly sensitive frequency detector across the whole signal: the detector will register high energy spikes exactly where the underlying, hidden sine waves reside, even if they are buried in noise. The math summarizes this energy distribution, revealing the spectrum of constituent frequencies regardless of how intermingled they appear in the time domain.",
      "source": "target",
